        "GENERATION 2 = the first run the #421 gating rule permits. gen-1's recipe unchanged with runJS2 and runJS3 added: 48,000 clusters of JS-M1-teacher self-play across runJS1-3, so 224,000 deals and 32,032,200 value records against gen-1's 192,000 and 27,602,516. The new corpus is 20.5% of the policy set against gen-1's 7.9% -- 2.6x the dilution, and the first rung where the pre-registered rule says a screen can resolve a plausible effect. Trained on the Mini 09-17 07:07 -> 14:36 ET, early-stopped at best epoch 8 of 11 run (JS-M1 17 of 20, gen-1 14 of 17): more rows per epoch, so patience on val_ce fires sooner. 653,532 params, parameter-matched with JS-M1 and gen-1, NOT compute-matched. val_ce 0.60998 IS CHARTED BUT MEASURED ON ITS OWN SPLIT (hollow dot, off the fitted lines), for the same reason as gen-1: the new corpora added ~10% of themselves to val, so val is 22,400 deals here against gen-1's 19,200 and JS-M1's 17,600. The monotone rise 0.59573 -> 0.60060 -> 0.60998 is measured on three nested, growing sets and the auxiliary points MAE rises with it (11.66 -> 11.85 -> 12.03) for three different models, which is the signature of set composition rather than model quality; on the FIXED holdouts the ordering does not hold (luna 0.84967 -> 0.84962 -> 0.84181, gen-2 best). OFFLINE, on the four FIXED external holdouts (identical rows, candidate digests verified equal), this is the FIRST generation to move in one direction -- top-1 roomlog 0.4656 -> 0.4755 -> 0.4796, luna 0.4567 -> 0.4552 -> 0.4664, highn 0.3746 -> 0.3788 -> 0.3793, best of the three on three of four; ranking regret roomlog 0.0705 and luna 0.0776, both best. gen-1 by contrast was a wash with no consistent direction. NO INTERVALS ARE ATTACHED to those point estimates and none should be quoted. The AUXILIARY points head degraded (roomlog MAE 14.590 -> 14.827, luna 16.884 -> 17.767) but the PRIMARY outcome head -- the one the search plays with -- is mixed, best of the three on luna (0.8418) and between the other two on roomlog; an earlier note of mine said the value head got worse, which named the wrong head. IN PLAY, SEALED 2026-09-18 00:11 ET under the pre-registration merged as #488 BEFORE these weights existed (lane v27 on the Mini, 15 windows x 520 clusters, seeds 16760910..17160910, capped 300 s, threshold 1,000, all three arms on the SAME tree and host so the contrasts are paired). BOTH CONTRASTS ARE NULL AND BOTH POINTS ARE NEGATIVE. CONTROL B, gen-2 minus PRODUCTION (the cumulative contrast, and the only one the triage rule reads): levels -0.0035 [-0.0252, +0.0183], RE SE 0.0111, tau 0, Q 0.29/4df, I2 0%, MDE80 0.0311 -- CROSSES ZERO; attacker points -0.3122, SE 0.2339, t(4) [-0.9615, +0.3371], p 0.254. CONTROL A, gen-2 minus GEN-1 (the #421 rule-2 incremental contrast): levels -0.0087 [-0.0301, +0.0126], tau 0, Q 0.10/4df, MDE80 0.0305; attacker points -0.4712, SE 0.2313, t(4) [-1.1133, +0.1709], p 0.111. THE POINTS INTERVALS ARE t(4), NOT NORMAL, AND THE DIFFERENCE DECIDES THE READING: on a normal approximation control A's points read [-0.9245, -0.0179] and would have EXCLUDED zero, i.e. gen-2 resolvably WORSE than gen-1; five windows give four degrees of freedom, and the correct t(4) interval crosses zero at p 0.111. Nothing here is resolvable. The pre-registered points hypothesis required the interval to exclude zero in the POSITIVE direction; it does not. Levels and points AGREE IN SIGN on both contrasts, which gen-1's two designs did not. Triage: control B's level point -0.0035 is far below the +0.015 extension line, so this is NOT extended to ten windows. Tie rates are unchanged and still dominate: 65.2% of paired deals tie on levels against 17.2% on points. READING, per #421 rule 4: this is the FIRST INFORMATIVE NULL -- the first rung at or above 20% new-teacher share -- so the question moves from 'is the corpus big enough' to whether the TEACHER must be STRONGER rather than merely more sampled. Readout archived at ~/shengji-archive/2026-09-13/readouts/v27-gen2-readout.txt.",
